From a1f7198f059e564a3474e2a693263820e5e93d0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 09:37:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [clangd] As an ok image, a module is rebuilt when a name its
 worker found absent exists

clang validates the inputs a BMI was built from (their size, time and
content), and nothing else. A header created after the BMI was built,
in a directory searched before the one whose header the BMI read, is not
one of its inputs: the BMI was reused, and the module went on reading
the header it would no longer include.

An ok image's module worker records what it reaches of the directories
it holds (openkal 0.23, KAL_DIR_RECORDED): it asks its C library to
attenuate each with a record (okm_preopen_attenuate, openkal-musl
0.27.0), in `inputs-<n>' of its unit, headed by the directory's name.
When the worker has ended, clangd takes the names its complete records
say were asked about and found absent, beneath no worker unit, and
writes them beside the BMI (`<bmi>.inputs', with the wall time before
the worker began) before the BMI is published; a BMI built without a
record has none. Before a BMI is reused, a name in that list that now
exists rebuilds it. A directory last changed before the worker began has
had no name added since, so a name is asked about only where its
directory changed; a check that held is not repeated within the recheck
interval while no change is reported, as validation verdicts are not.

The owned cache knows the list as a payload of its generation, and a
worker unit knows its records.
